node_modules/
# npm rotates a debug log to `npm-debug.log.1`, `npm-debug.log.2026-10-17` or
# `npm-debug.log.gz`: the name is kept and the ending is not `.log`, so `*.log`
# below misses exactly the logs most likely to be the long ones.
*.log
*.log.*
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store
# `npm pack` writes the tarball it reports into the working directory.
*.tgz

# Working notes for this fork's own review rounds, kept out of the tree.
/zenfree-audit/

# Secrets. Nothing here is written by this repository, and none of it belongs in
# a commit: the npm token MAINTAINING.md describes lives in an npmrc outside it.
# The log patterns above are deliberately unanchored, because npm's debug logs
# record the whole command line and a log written from a subdirectory is the
# same leak. `*.log.*` and `npm-debug.log*` are that rule extended to the names a
# rotation leaves, because npm renames a log rather than rewriting it — a rotated
# log still holds the command line, it has just stopped ending in `.log`.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.npmrc
# The publish recipe in MAINTAINING.md writes its token to a file named `npmrc`,
# which `.npmrc` does not match.
npmrc
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
*.der
id_rsa*
# The public halves are not secrets, and a fixture or a deploy note legitimately
# carries one: `id_rsa*` above swallows them, so they are put back by name.
!id_rsa.pub
id_ed25519*
!id_ed25519.pub
# The rest of `ssh-keygen`'s types, and PuTTY's own key file — the one most
# likely to be sitting in the working directory on the Windows setup this
# project targets.
id_ecdsa*
!id_ecdsa.pub
id_dsa*
!id_dsa.pub
*.ppk
